00:00:17.100 --> 00:00:19.470
Networks are at the
foundation of an environment.

00:00:19.470 --> 00:00:21.210
You need a pane of
glass that can provide

00:00:21.210 --> 00:00:24.150
a multitude of tools to
troubleshoot, diagnose,

00:00:24.150 --> 00:00:25.890
and monitor your environment.

00:00:25.890 --> 00:00:28.920
The Network Intelligence Center
provides that single console

00:00:28.920 --> 00:00:32.220
for managing Google Cloud
network visibility, monitoring,

00:00:32.220 --> 00:00:34.320
verification, and optimization.

00:00:34.320 --> 00:00:36.570
Today, there are currently
four modules in the Network

00:00:36.570 --> 00:00:37.470
Intelligence Center--

00:00:37.470 --> 00:00:41.280
network topology, connectivity
tests, performance dashboard,

00:00:41.280 --> 00:00:42.660
and firewall insights.

00:00:42.660 --> 00:00:44.400
Today, we'll be covering
new enhancements

00:00:44.400 --> 00:00:47.700
for the connectivity test and
the performance dashboard.

00:00:47.700 --> 00:00:49.530
Diving right in,
connectivity test

00:00:49.530 --> 00:00:51.570
is a configuration
and route analyzer

00:00:51.570 --> 00:00:54.330
that lets you diagnose
connectivity issues in your VPC

00:00:54.330 --> 00:00:56.580
network due to a
misconfiguration

00:00:56.580 --> 00:00:58.500
or due to missing routes.

00:00:58.500 --> 00:01:00.600
When analyzing network
configurations,

00:01:00.600 --> 00:01:03.270
connectivity test uses
an abstract state machine

00:01:03.270 --> 00:01:06.390
to model how a VPC network
should process packets.

00:01:06.390 --> 00:01:08.040
Google Cloud
processes the packet

00:01:08.040 --> 00:01:10.250
in several logical steps.

00:01:10.250 --> 00:01:13.100
Previously, connectivity test
was purely a configuration

00:01:13.100 --> 00:01:14.090
analyzer.

00:01:14.090 --> 00:01:17.120
With the launch of dynamic
reachability verification,

00:01:17.120 --> 00:01:20.240
we introduced live
data plane analysis.

00:01:20.240 --> 00:01:22.610
Dynamic verification
tests connectivity

00:01:22.610 --> 00:01:25.010
by sending multiple trace
packets from the source

00:01:25.010 --> 00:01:27.320
endpoint to the destination.

00:01:27.320 --> 00:01:29.270
The dynamic verification
results show

00:01:29.270 --> 00:01:31.280
you the number of
probes sent, the number

00:01:31.280 --> 00:01:33.920
of probes that successfully
reached the destination,

00:01:33.920 --> 00:01:36.020
and a reachability status.

00:01:36.020 --> 00:01:38.360
The status is determined
based on how many probes

00:01:38.360 --> 00:01:40.490
were successfully delivered.

00:01:40.490 --> 00:01:43.670
Reachability is more than 95%.

00:01:43.670 --> 00:01:45.980
Unreachable is 0%.

00:01:45.980 --> 00:01:49.430
And partially reachable
is between 0% and 95%.

00:01:49.430 --> 00:01:51.950
In addition to showing how
many packets were successfully

00:01:51.950 --> 00:01:54.140
delivered, dynamic
verification also

00:01:54.140 --> 00:01:58.370
shows median and 95th percentile
one-way latency information.

00:01:58.370 --> 00:02:01.070
The last topic today will be
the performance dashboard.

00:02:01.070 --> 00:02:02.990
Performance dashboard
gives you the visibility

00:02:02.990 --> 00:02:05.570
into the performance of
your project's resources,

00:02:05.570 --> 00:02:08.150
as well as the performance
of the entire Google Cloud

00:02:08.150 --> 00:02:09.289
network.

00:02:09.289 --> 00:02:12.180
It will provide you with packet
loss and latency details.

00:02:12.180 --> 00:02:13.070
So how does it work?

00:02:13.070 --> 00:02:14.780
Performance dashboard
runs workers

00:02:14.780 --> 00:02:17.870
on physical hosts
that house your VMs.

00:02:17.870 --> 00:02:20.150
These workers insert and
receive probe packets

00:02:20.150 --> 00:02:22.400
that run on the same
network as your traffic.

00:02:22.400 --> 00:02:24.890
Because the workers run on
the physical host and not

00:02:24.890 --> 00:02:28.490
on the VMs, these workers
do not consume VM resources

00:02:28.490 --> 00:02:31.310
and the traffic is not
visible to your VMs.

00:02:31.310 --> 00:02:32.780
What about latency?

00:02:32.780 --> 00:02:36.440
For latency, latency is
measured by TCP packets.

00:02:36.440 --> 00:02:38.540
Based on the sample of
your actual traffic,

00:02:38.540 --> 00:02:40.850
latency is calculated
as the time that

00:02:40.850 --> 00:02:43.460
elapsed between sending
the TCP sequence number

00:02:43.460 --> 00:02:45.500
and receiving the
corresponding ACK that

00:02:45.500 --> 00:02:49.430
contains network RTT and
TCP stack-related delay.

00:02:49.430 --> 00:02:51.710
The UI shows latency
at the median of all

00:02:51.710 --> 00:02:53.360
the relevant measurements.

00:02:53.360 --> 00:02:55.880
In the project performance
view, performance dashboard

00:02:55.880 --> 00:02:58.280
shows packet loss
and latency for zones

00:02:58.280 --> 00:03:01.310
where you have Compute Engine
resources for your project.

00:03:01.310 --> 00:03:03.770
This release introduces
a performance dashboard

00:03:03.770 --> 00:03:05.390
for all of Google Cloud.

00:03:05.390 --> 00:03:08.030
The dashboard presents the
overall Google Cloud metrics

00:03:08.030 --> 00:03:11.570
for each possible
Google Cloud zone pair.

00:03:11.570 --> 00:03:14.150
It displays the current
status of Google Cloud Network

00:03:14.150 --> 00:03:17.150
and lets you compare performance
across all of Google Cloud

00:03:17.150 --> 00:03:20.520
to the performance
observed in your projects.

00:03:20.520 --> 00:03:22.680
Similar to project
view, performance

00:03:22.680 --> 00:03:25.200
will be shown via summary
charts and heat maps

00:03:25.200 --> 00:03:27.150
for both packet
loss and latency.

00:03:27.150 --> 00:03:30.390
And you have the ability to
review six weeks of history.

00:03:30.390 --> 00:03:32.670
So that's all we have for
this episode of "What's

00:03:32.670 --> 00:03:33.625
New in Networking."

